  "text": "Earlier work from this group showed that mixed bacterial communities can consume different food sources one after another when the food is abundant. The present experiments demonstrate that the same step‑wise consumption also happens when the food is present at very low levels—about five milligrams per litre of a common carbohydrate and five milligrams per litre of a related sugar alcohol—under the conditions of a routine test that measures how much oxygen the microbes use. The oxygen‑use curve displayed two distinct rising sections separated by a clear flat interval, indicating a pause between the two phases of consumption. This pattern provides a plausible reason for the sudden changes sometimes observed in the rate at which carbon‑based oxygen demand is expressed during such tests."
